  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1138 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nTonight through Wednesday\n\nHigh pressure will provide dry weather through Wednesday. The\nHRRR indicates some light concentrations of smoke near the\nsurface (probably too low to be noticed) and much greater smoke\nconcentration around 6K feet. The latter will likely produce a \nmilky appearance to the sky. Low temperatures will range from \naround 60 to the mid-60s tonight. High temperatures will be in \nthe 80s.\n\nWednesday Night\n\nA weak shortwave trough will move east through Minnesota and\nWisconsin on Wednesday night. The 0-1 km ML CAPES will be up \n250 J/kg. This should be enough instability for some showers and\nstorms to develop mainly north of Interstate 94. With the 0-3 \nkm and 0-6 km shear being generally less than 20 knots, \norganized severe weather is not anticipated. Rainfall totals \nwill be generally less than a tenth of an inch. Low temperatures\nwill be in the mid- and upper 60s.\n\nThursday\n\nA ridge of high pressure will provide dry weather across the \narea on Thursday. High temperatures will be in the 80s.\n\nThursday into Saturday\n\nA slow-moving low pressure system will slowly move east through\nthe Upper Mississippi River Valley. Many of the models have \nshowers and storms across the area from Thursday night into \nFriday night. Some of these models suggest that this rain will \ncontinue into Saturday. Precipitable water values generally \nrange from 1.5 to around 2 inches. Some of models are even up \nto 2.5 inches. Warm cloud layer depths are generally around 4 \nkm. This will result in the potential for highly efficient rain \nproducing showers and storms. Rainfall amounts look to be in the\n1 to 2-inch range. The LREF suggests that there is up to 15 \npercent chance of rain totals over 2 inches. \n\nWith 0-1 km mixed-layer CAPES only up to 500 J/kg and weak to\nmoderate 0-3 km and 0-6 km shear (generally less than 30 knots),\norganized severe convection looks unlikely at this time.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the mid- and upper 70s on Friday\nand around 80 on Saturday. Low temperatures will be in the mid-\nand upper 60s on Thursday night and around 60 on Friday night. \n\nSaturday night into Monday\n\nHigh pressure will build across the area on Saturday night and\npersist into early next week. High temperatures will range from\nthe upper 70s to mid-80s on Sunday and in the lower and mid-80s\non Monday. Low temperatures will range from the mid-50s to lower\n60s on Saturday night and around 60 on Monday night.",
